1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,817
This is ScopePay. It turns a messy client
brief into a reviewed, priced scope, and

2
00:00:05,875 --> 00:00:12,167
links that exact version to a payment on
PayPal's sandbox. Sandbox — so nothing you

3
00:00:12,249 --> 00:00:14,013
see here moves real money.

4
00:00:15,151 --> 00:00:20,352
It starts with the brief: five pages, copy
that needs a tidy-up, and it has to work

5
00:00:20,422 --> 00:00:27,701
on phones. An Apertus model reads that and
proposes which catalog items are in scope.

6
00:00:28,143 --> 00:00:33,994
It never proposes a price. The server
re-prices from its own fixed catalog, and

7
00:00:34,064 --> 00:00:39,439
anything the model cannot justify becomes
an ambiguity a human has to settle.

8
00:00:40,589 --> 00:00:45,407
The merchant reviews it. Their edit is
authoritative, and it produces a new

9
00:00:45,476 --> 00:00:50,678
version that can never be rewritten,
sealed with a hash over the exact scope.

10
00:00:51,189 --> 00:00:55,821
That hash is what the buyer will approve —
and that is what makes a stale scope

11
00:00:55,903 --> 00:00:56,994
unpayable.

12
00:00:58,085 --> 00:01:02,938
Before anything reaches PayPal, a
deterministic gate re-checks four things

13
00:01:03,031 --> 00:01:07,908
on the server: the version hash, the
amount, the currency, and the merchant.

14
00:01:08,418 --> 00:01:13,585
The model has no authority to move money
here. If the hash is stale, or an amount

15
00:01:13,643 --> 00:01:16,871
was tampered with, the request stops at
this gate.

16
00:01:18,275 --> 00:01:22,791
Here is the whole trust boundary. The
browser holds only publishable

17
00:01:22,826 --> 00:01:27,888
configuration. The server owns the
catalog, the pricing and the gate, and it

18
00:01:27,946 --> 00:01:33,391
holds the credentials. PayPal alone
decides whether money moved. The model

19
00:01:33,449 --> 00:01:35,632
proposes; it never pays.

20
00:01:36,689 --> 00:01:41,437
The buyer approves on PayPal's own page,
and the server reads the order back and

21
00:01:41,553 --> 00:01:47,498
captures it. Order zero S L zero five
seven three eight S seven two six two

22
00:01:47,579 --> 00:01:54,081
three zero four T; sixteen hundred and
forty US dollars; capture id six Y S two

23
00:01:54,197 --> 00:02:00,791
eight six nine six two F seven two three
two one zero Y. PayPal reports: completed.

24
00:02:01,116 --> 00:02:06,259
The receipt keeps what PayPal reported.
What is still unproven is on the record

25
00:02:06,317 --> 00:02:11,948
too: a webhook delivery was verified
against PayPal's own verifier, but no

26
00:02:12,041 --> 00:02:16,836
signed event has yet applied a state
change, and timeouts and declines are

27
00:02:16,917 --> 00:02:18,159
untested.
